the u.s and china have announced a plan  to work together on cutting their  emissions in the next decade the world's  two biggest greenhouse gas emitters  unveiled an agreement at the un climate  summit in scotland that includes plans  to reduce methane output phase out coal  consumption and protect forests though  light on solid commitments the pact  could send a strong signal to some 200  other countries at the cop 26 climate  talks  announcing the agreement u.s special  envoy john kerry said china and the u.s  may have their differences but  cooperation on climate was imperative we  also expressed a shared desire for  success at this cop  on mitigation  adaptation  support  and frankly all of the key issues  which will result in the world raising  ambition and being able to address this  crisis  now with this announcement we've arrived  at a new step  a road map for our present  and future collaboration on this issue  so john kerry says the u.s and china now  have a road map for taking action  chinese leaders have been noticeably  absent from cop 26 but china does have a  chief climate negotiator there and he  also spoke of the important role the two  countries share  china and the us shoulder special  international responsibilities and  obligations  we need to think big and be responsible  and we need to work in line with the  global trend and work hard to promote  world peace and development  we need to actively address climate  change and through cooperation bring  more benefit to both our two peoples and  people around the world  cutting coal will be key to china's  emissions reduction targets but the  country is still expanding its use of  the fossil fuel most of the country's  energy comes from coal and china is the  world's largest producer and consumer of  it  our next report is from the city of  datung in changshi province which is  considered the capital of coal mining in  china  at first sight there's a sea of wind  turbines blanketing the surrounding  terrain  but this is dartong china's coal capital  the city is so steeped in the industry  it even has a museum dedicated to its  history  people have been mining coal in dartong  for nearly 1500 years  but as china tries to rid itself of  fossil fuel generated energy  many here are uncertain what the future  holds for them and for the mines  maybe there won't be any in the future  how would so many workers and their  families survive without coal  lee is a miner in dartong like several  generations before him he lives from  coal production  his son has followed in his footsteps  like in dartong some 900 mines in shanxi  province employ hundreds of thousands of  people  for many it's hard to imagine a life  without them  where can we go then i have no other  skills and benefits i can't live  anywhere else if i'm going to die i'm  going to die here there's no work to do  in other places  for now workers aren't too concerned  china's struggling to shake off its coal  addiction and there's little sign of  those jobs drying up  china's ramped up investment in wind and  solar energy in recent years but it's  still heavily reliant on coal to meet  its massive power demands  coal makes up around 60 of the country's  total energy use and over the last  decade it has consumed more of the fuel  than the rest of the world combined  last month authorities ordered mines to  boost coal production by nearly 100  million tons to battle a power crunch  threatening china's economic recovery  from the coronavirus crisis it's also  needed to ease an expected energy supply  shortage for winter  china has pledged that coal consumption  will peak by 2025  but drastic reliance on the fuel source  to meet energy demands has raised doubts  about whether that's feasible  what will happen after after the peak  is as important as when the peak will  happen  are we going to see a long plateau and  if so that's probably not great news for  climate change or are we seeing a steady  decline or a drastic decline if china  fails to cut coal out of its energy mix  in time it will be all the more  difficult to achieve its bigger goal of  net zero emissions by 2060.  i'm joined now by julio friedman from  the center on global energy policy at  columbia university dr friedman is one  of the leading experts on carbon removal  dr fredman welcome china is a major  polluter as we've just heard heavily  dependent on coal how optimistic are you  that its agreement announced that cop26  to boost climate cooperation with the us  will yield the results that are needed  i think that this announcement is a  triumph and i know that the  diplomats and state department officials  working with their counterparts in china  have been discussing this for seven  months  uh and it is a necessary step in order  to get the world to a net zero economy  so much of the focus is understandably  here on what governments can do to  reduce carbon emissions but can carbon  capture technology on the other side of  this equation really be effective in  tackling the problem  absolutely  it's noteworthy that among the topics  highlighted in the u.s china agreement  carbon capture technology was one that  was singled out and the reason why is  because we cannot reach our net zero  commitments without it neither the u.s  nor china or many other countries  and not just for the power sector really  for the heavy industrial sector is where  carbon capture technology will be  important  another thing that was highlighted in  the announcement was direct air capture  which is one of the many ways you can  remove co2 from the air and oceans  that technology  is beginning to enter the marketplace  but again requires a great deal more  investigation and support but the  science is clear and the technology  works  so but the technology to remove carbon  from the oceans and the atmosphere is in  its infancy as you say you said that  this needs to grow as an industry that  to be twice as big as the oil and gas  sector within 30 years is that realistic  who pays for it and if it is realistic  on that time scale wouldn't that that  not be too late for us  well we know the recipe to get this done  we've done the exact same thing with led  lights we've done the exact same thing  with batteries we've done the exact same  thing with wind and solar  the recipe is a combination of  innovation  and market aligning policies and private  investment  and those are all beginning to happen in  this space whether or not we get there  in enough time is really up to us but  there is nothing in the physics or  chemistry or politics that naturally  prevents this outcome  what are the risks of carbon capture and  storage as we look further down the line  could using co2 storing co2 underground  just leave the problem for future  generations when we don't know what that  cost will be  uh i think that is a misunderstanding of  what carbon capture is and how it works  um it is a radical departure from  business as usual  uh and in point of fact we have been  putting co2 in geological formations  with zero incident and with zero leakage  for over 50 years  uh it's quite the opposite actually not  doing this technology path in fact will  unquestionably burden present and future  generations uh if we take direct action  now the earth will eventually mineralize  all the co2  and  essentially  return  the carbon to the earth exactly  where it came from  dr friedman from columbia university  center on global energy policy thanks  for your time  my pleasure thank you  you
 

